#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Duty Board v3.117.0 — Accounts 5: Tax Accounting & Nigerian Compliance
(pass 5 of 8).

The money-heavy module, ten chapters: the custody thesis consolidated,
the Nigerian tax frame (NTA 2025 context), VAT both sides, WHT both
directions, PAYE and payroll statutories, the filing and remittance
rituals, the compliance calendar, the audit evidence posture, the
compliance year, and the fifth law. Manual depth from birth:
10 chapters, 35-bank.

Deploy: apply -> commit -> then on the server:
  bench --site xlevel.clouderp.one execute duty_board.academy_seed_accounts_pro.seed_accounts_pro_track

Anchored, idempotent. Requires v3.116.0.
Run from ~/frappe-bench/apps/duty_board.
"""

import io
import json
import os
import sys

INIT = "duty_board/__init__.py"
DATA_PATH = "duty_board/academy_accounts_pro_data.json"
SEEDER_PATH = "duty_board/academy_seed_accounts_pro.py"
CHECK_ONLY = "--check" in sys.argv

L = lambda t, est, html: {"title": t, "est": est, "html": html}
Q = lambda q, opts, ans, why, src: {"q": q, "opts": opts, "ans": ans, "why": why, "src": src}

LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The silent partner: custody consolidated", "13", "<p>Three tracks met the tax accounts one at a time — output VAT at the invoice (Selling 6), input VAT and WHT at the bill (Procurement 7), PAYE at the payroll journal (module 2). This module consolidates them into what they jointly are: <b>the state's wallet inside the firm's chart</b> — and the discipline of a firm that holds another party's money professionally.</p><p><b>The custody thesis, stated once for everything.</b> Tax money in a trading firm is overwhelmingly money the firm HOLDS, not money it OWES from its own pocket: the VAT charged on every invoice was collected FROM customers FOR the state; the WHT deducted from a supplier's payment is the supplier's tax, held in transit; PAYE is the employees' income tax, withheld at source. The grammar makes it visible (these are all CREDITS to liability accounts — module 1's ₦7,500 line), and the operational consequence is the module's spine: <b>custody money is not revenue, not cash flow, and not available</b> — the firm that treats its VAT account as working capital is spending someone else's money, and remittance day arrives as a crisis precisely as often as that treatment is habitual. Chapter 7's reserve discipline is the cure; this chapter's job is that the disease is understood as what it is.</p><p><b>The wallet's compartments.</b> The custody and near-custody accounts a trading firm carries: <b>VAT Payable</b> (output custody) and <b>Input VAT</b> (the claimable asset that offsets it — Chapter 3 joins them); <b>WHT Payable</b> (custody as deducting agent) and <b>WHT Receivable</b> (the mirror — the firm's OWN tax prepaid via customers' deductions, an asset with a chase attached — Chapter 4); the <b>payroll custodies</b> (PAYE, pension, and the statutory family — Chapter 5); and beside them the firm's own tax liabilities proper (companies income tax and its kin — provision-style accounts the accountant owns, distinguished exactly because they ARE the firm's own cost, not custody). Every compartment is an account (module 1's types), fed by documents and templates (the posting map), reconciled to a register, and cleared by a remittance ritual — one pattern, five appearances, which is why one module can hold them all.</p><p><b>Compliance as rhythm, not emergency.</b> The module's cultural argument, previewed: Nigerian tax compliance for a documented trading firm is a CALENDAR — monthly returns and remittances on fixed days, annual filings on fixed horizons, each fed by registers the system already keeps. The firm that experiences compliance as recurring emergency has not met a harsher authority than its neighbours; it has declined the rhythm — filings reconstructed from spreadsheets at deadline, custody money discovered spent, certificates never collected. The consultant's product in this module is the boring version: registers that tie, accounts that zero, a calendar with names, and an evidence file that answers queries in a day (Chapter 8). Boring, as three tracks have established, is the certificate.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Nigerian frame: the landscape, for consultants", "14", "<p>The machinery is universal; the landscape is Nigerian — and a consultant implementing for Nigerian trading firms needs the map at working altitude: what taxes touch the client, who collects them, what the 2025 reform reshaped, and where the consultant's competence ends. This chapter is that map — deliberately conceptual, because rates and thresholds are the accountant's living knowledge, not a manual's frozen table.</p><p><b>The taxes that touch a trading firm.</b> The working set: <b>VAT</b> — the consumption tax on supplies, collected and remitted monthly (Chapter 3); <b>WHT</b> — the withholding mechanism riding specified payment types, both directions (Chapter 4); <b>PAYE</b> — employees' income tax withheld monthly, remitted to the STATE authority of the employee's residence (the federal/state split that surprises new consultants: VAT and companies income tax run federal; PAYE runs to states — one firm, several authorities, one calendar); <b>Companies Income Tax</b> — the firm's own annual tax on profits, provisioned through the year and the accountant's core territory; and the <b>statutory family</b> around payroll (pension, housing, insurance, training levies — Chapter 5's concepts). Each has its authority, its rhythm, and its account — and the consultant's competence is knowing WHICH machinery serves each, not computing any of them.</p><p><b>The 2025 reform, at consultant altitude.</b> The Nigeria Tax Act 2025 and its companion acts consolidated a scattered statute book and restructured administration — the working consequences a consultant should hold: the tax authority landscape reorganised (the federal service restructured under new legislation, with the system's registrations and filing rails updated accordingly), thresholds and reliefs reshaped (small-company regimes, rate structures, and exemption bands moved — the accountant re-reads eligibility annually, and the consultant's contribution is that the system can APPLY whatever the current answer is: templates adjustable, categories flaggable), and the compliance infrastructure pushed further digital — e-filing as the norm, and <b>e-invoicing/fiscalisation</b> advancing (Selling 6 taught the merchant-facing reality; the accounts chair's addition is that the invoice register and the filed return increasingly reconcile AUTOMATICALLY on the authority's side — which converts register-to-account discipline from good practice into survival, because the authority now holds one half of the reconciliation).</p><p><b>The consultant's boundary, drawn once for the module.</b> The Inventory 5 boundary, tax edition, and it governs every chapter ahead: <b>the machinery is yours; the advice is theirs.</b> Yours: the templates that compute correctly, the registers that tie to accounts, the rituals and calendar installed, the evidence file structured, the system's categories able to express whatever treatment the accountant directs. Theirs: what rate applies to this transaction, whether this firm qualifies for that regime, how this deduction interacts with that relief, what position to take when the law is unclear. The consultant who answers rate-and-eligibility questions is practising tax without a licence and assuming liability without insurance; the one who says <i>the system can do whichever your tax adviser directs — here is how</i> is doing the job at its professional grade. This sentence recurs because clients will test it monthly.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — VAT consolidated: both sides, one position", "14", "<p>Two tracks each held half; the accounts chair holds the whole: output and input joined into the monthly VAT position, and the ritual that files it. The consolidation chapter.</p><p><b>The two sides, recapped in one breath.</b> OUTPUT (Selling 6): every taxable sale charges VAT via the template — CR VAT Payable, the custody growing with trade, the invoice register its document-level mirror. INPUT (Procurement 7): VAT paid on qualifying purchases — DR Input VAT, an asset, the claim's evidence being the supplier's proper invoice (the discipline that made invoice quality a money issue: ₦100-with-input beats ₦99-without). The chart carries both compartments (module 1's typed accounts), the documents feed both automatically, and the chair's monthly work is joining them.</p><p><b>The net position.</b> The month's arithmetic: output charged minus allowable input equals the position — PAYABLE when output exceeds input (the normal trading month: remit the net), a CARRY-FORWARD credit when input exceeds output (the heavy-buying month, the import-stocking quarter — the credit rides to offset future output, with refund mechanics existing in principle but carry-forward being the practical road). The system computes it because the documents fed it; the return states it; and the reconciliation BEFORE filing is the ritual's heart: <b>the invoice register's output total ties to the VAT Payable account's movement; the purchase register's input total ties to the Input VAT account's movement</b> — the two register-to-account bridges both money tracks installed, now run as one pre-filing checklist line, because a return filed from numbers the ledger cannot reproduce is Chapter 10's pattern wearing a stamp.</p><p><b>The classification edges.</b> Where VAT literacy earns its keep — the categories the templates must express: <b>standard-rated</b> supplies (the default trading reality), <b>zero-rated</b> (taxable at zero — the distinction that matters because zero-rated supplies still belong in the return and preserve input claims on their costs), <b>exempt</b> (outside the charge — and the subtle consequence the accountant will care about: input VAT attributable to exempt supplies is generally not claimable, so a mixed-supply firm — the pharmacy whose basic-medicine lines ride an exemption while its cosmetics shelf is standard-rated — needs its ITEMS tax-categorised correctly at the master level so the registers can tell the streams apart; the apportionment arithmetic is the accountant's, the categorisation machinery is yours), and the special regimes the accountant flags (imports' VAT at clearing — Procurement 6's landed file; reverse-charge concepts on certain imported services). The item-master tax category is where all of this becomes operational — one more master-data field with a decade of consequences, set at creation, audited annually.</p><p><b>The monthly VAT ritual, end to end.</b> Register closed for the month (both sides); registers reconciled to accounts (the two bridges, to the kobo, divergences worked before — never after — filing); the return prepared FROM the system's numbers (the return is a restatement of the ledger, not a parallel computation); filed by the calendar's date; the net remitted; and the accounts cleared — the payment entry that empties VAT Payable (net of the input offset) against the bank, leaving the custody at its post-remittance level (zero, or the carry-forward, exactly). The invariant Chapter 6 generalises debuts here: <b>after a clean cycle, the custody account's balance is explainable in one sentence</b> — and any residue that isn't is next month's first worked item.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — WHT both directions: agent and sufferer", "14", "<p>Withholding tax is the mechanism trading firms most often hold wrong, because it runs in BOTH directions at once and each direction has a different accounting life. The chair's consolidated treatment.</p><p><b>As agent: deducting from suppliers.</b> Procurement 7's machinery, recapped as custody: qualifying payments (services, rent, professional fees, contracts — the TYPES are set by law; the current rates and thresholds are the accountant's living table) are settled net, the deduction credited to <b>WHT Payable</b> — the supplier's tax, held: the ₦1m professional invoice settled as ₦950k cash plus ₦50k custody. The agent's obligations complete the picture: the custody REMITTED monthly by the calendar (Chapter 6's ritual — the account zeroing after), and the <b>certificates</b> procured and delivered to the suppliers whose money was withheld — because the certificate is the supplier's evidence for THEIR tax credit, and the agent who deducts but never delivers certificates is quietly injuring every supplier it pays (the relationship cost) while accumulating an administrative debt (the compliance cost). The deduction-detail register — who, what, when, how much — is the remittance schedule's source and the certificate run's checklist, one register serving both.</p><p><b>As sufferer: customers deducting from you.</b> The mirror, and the direction firms neglect at real cost: corporate and government customers deduct WHT from THEIR payments to the firm — the ₦1m receivable settling as ₦950k cash plus a ₦50k deduction. The accounting: the deduction is NOT a discount, a loss, or a mystery shortpay (Chapter 2 of module 4's taxonomy at work) — it is the firm's OWN income tax, prepaid: DR <b>WHT Receivable</b> (an asset) in the settlement entry, the receivable's evidence being the customer's certificate. Two disciplines make the asset real: the <b>capture</b> (every deduction recognised as WHT at allocation — the shortpay coded correctly, the asset built transaction by transaction) and the <b>certificate chase</b> (the standing worklist: deductions taken, certificates outstanding, aged per customer — chased with the same professionalism as any receivable, because an uncertificated deduction is money in limbo: suffered but unprovable). The asset's destiny closes the loop: WHT suffered offsets the firm's income tax liability — the credit the accountant applies at filing — which is why the chase MATTERS in naira: every uncollected certificate is the firm's own tax paid twice, once by deduction and once at assessment.</p><p><b>The both-directions month.</b> A trading firm of any size runs both lives simultaneously: deducting from its service suppliers while being deducted from by its corporate customers — two accounts (one liability, one asset), two registers, two worklists (the remittance schedule and the certificate chase), never netted (module 4's law verbatim: different assertions, different destinies). The monthly ritual runs both: agent side — register closed, tied to WHT Payable, remitted, zeroed, certificates dispatched; sufferer side — deductions captured, certificates chased, the receivable's aging read, the accountant handed the period's asset position for the CIT picture. Twenty minutes each when the disciplines hold; the alternative — both directions run on memory — is the module's most reliably expensive neglect, and Chapter 9's case study prices it in recovered naira.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — PAYE & the payroll custodies", "13", "<p>The payroll journal (module 2) credited a row of custody accounts in one line; this chapter opens the row — the employee-money custodies, their rhythms, and the tie that keeps payroll defensible.</p><p><b>The custody family.</b> The monthly payroll carves an employee's gross into destinations, and each destination is an account with an authority behind it: <b>PAYE</b> — the employee's income tax, withheld under the current law's bands and reliefs (the 2025 reform reshaped the bands and the low-income threshold — the accountant's living table; the system's contribution is a payroll setup that applies whatever the current structure is), remitted monthly to the STATE of the employee's residence (the multi-state workforce means multi-state remittance — a Lagos head office with Ogun-resident staff files in both, and the payroll register must split by state to serve it); <b>pension</b> — the contributory scheme's employee and employer portions, remitted to each employee's chosen fund administrator (plural administrators, one schedule — the register splits by PFA); and the <b>levy family</b> the accountant confirms applicability of by headcount and sector — housing fund, insurance trust, industrial training — each a small account with its own rhythm. The employer's OWN portions (pension employer share, the levies borne by the firm) are expense, not custody — the distinction the grammar makes automatic and the reader should hold: withheld-from-employee is custody; borne-by-employer is cost.</p><p><b>The payroll tie.</b> The register-to-account bridge, payroll edition: the payroll register's column totals (gross, PAYE, pension by portion, net pay) tie to the payroll journal's lines, which tie to the custody accounts' movements, which zero at remittance — one chain, checked monthly in minutes, and the chain's health is what makes every payroll question answerable: the employee disputing a deduction (the register's row, the payslip's mirror), the authority querying a remittance (the schedule, the receipt), the auditor sampling a month (the chain, walked). The payslip discipline rides here: every employee receives the statement of their own carving — gross, deductions itemised, net — every month, because the payslip is the employee's certificate (the WHT logic, personal edition) and the firm's first defence in any deduction dispute.</p><p><b>The rhythms and the year.</b> Monthly: PAYE remitted by the calendar's day (state by state), pension within its statutory window (fund by fund), the custodies zeroing after each. Annually: the employer's returns — the annual PAYE return declaring every employee's year (filed to each relevant state), and the reconciliations the accountant runs between the twelve monthly filings and the annual declaration. The system's job throughout is the register that can produce ANY slice on demand — by month, by employee, by state, by fund — because every payroll obligation is a different slice of one dataset, and a firm whose payroll lives in the system answers all of them from one place. The firm whose payroll lives in a spreadsheet beside the system answers none of them without archaeology — and reconciles the spreadsheet to the GL never, which is Chapter 10's filing-from-outside pattern wearing its most common face.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The filing & remittance ritual: the zeroing invariant", "13", "<p>Every custody chapter ended at the same machinery; here it is once, generalised — the monthly ritual that turns tax from anxiety into procedure, and the invariant that proves each cycle clean.</p><p><b>The ritual's six steps, for any custody.</b> <b>Close the register</b> — the month's document-level detail (invoices for VAT, deductions for WHT, the payroll register for PAYE) cut off at the period, because the register is the return's evidence and the account's mirror. <b>Reconcile register to account</b> — the bridge, to the kobo, divergences worked NOW: the register-account disagreement found before filing is a bookkeeping task; found after, it is an amended return, an authority conversation, or both. <b>Prepare the return FROM the system</b> — the return restates the ledger; the parallel spreadsheet computation is the pattern this module keeps convicting, because two computations guarantee a divergence and the filed one wins the argument with the authority while the booked one wins it with the auditor: one source, or two problems. <b>File by the date</b> — the calendar's line (Chapter 7), owned by name. <b>Remit</b> — the payment entry, from the reserve (Chapter 7's discipline), referenced to the filing. <b>Verify the zero</b> — the invariant: <b>after a clean cycle, each custody account returns to its explainable level</b> — zero for the fully-remitted custodies, the stated carry-forward for VAT credit months — and any unexplained residue is next month's first investigation, because a custody account that will not zero is telling one of a short list of stories: a register-account divergence (the bridge skipped), a remittance short or misposted (the payment against the wrong account), a prior month's residue compounding (the drift that becomes archaeology), or custody spent (Chapter 1's disease, surfacing as arithmetic).</p><p><b>The evidence file.</b> Each cycle files its bundle: the register extract, the reconciliation, the return as filed, the filing acknowledgment, the remittance evidence, the zero-verification — one folder per tax per month, boring and complete. This is Chapter 8's audit posture being built monthly as a by-product (the academy's oldest economics: the system that runs well IS the system that evidences well), and its absence is why authority queries paralyse unprepared firms: the answer exists but the assembling is a week, and a week's silence reads as evasion when a day's reply reads as competence.</p><p><b>The penalties frame — why late is expensive, without the rate table.</b> The consultant's framing for owners, numbers left to the accountant: Nigerian tax penalties compound along three axes — the fixed late-filing penalties (per return, accruing monthly), interest on unremitted amounts (running from the due date), and the escalation costs (the audit attention a chronically-late filer invites, the tax clearance certificate — needed for contracts, banking, and bids — delayed or denied). The arithmetic the framing produces: the cost of the RITUAL is a few hours monthly; the cost of its absence is penalties plus interest plus the commercial doors a missing clearance closes — and the comparison is not close. Compliance's best-kept secret, taught to every client: the authorities are overwhelmingly manageable for the firm that files on time from records that tie; nearly all compliance pain concentrates on the firms that force reconstruction — and which side of that line a firm lives on is decided by this chapter's six steps, run monthly, owned by name.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The compliance calendar & the reserve", "13", "<p>The rituals need their WHEN and their MONEY. The consolidated calendar, and the reserve discipline that makes remittance day a transfer instead of a crisis.</p><p><b>The calendar, consolidated.</b> The monthly spine — the recurring dates a trading firm lives by (the specific statutory days are the accountant's confirmation; the SHAPE is universal): the VAT return and remittance in the month's third week for the prior month; WHT remittance on its earlier track (the agent's custody moves faster — typically the third week for federal, with state-collected streams on their own days); PAYE to each relevant state by its mid-month day; pension within days of paying salaries (the tightest window in the family — statutorily pinned to the payment of wages, not the month's end). The annual layer rides above: companies income tax filing on the firm's filing horizon (months after year-end, the accountant's flagship), the annual PAYE employer returns in the new year's first month, and the levy family's annual cycles. The calendar is built ONCE per client — every obligation, its authority, its day, its OWNER, its evidence-file location — and it lives where every calendar in this academy lives: on the policy page, reviewed annually and at every legal change, with module 8 slotting it into the finance function's master rhythm.</p><p><b>The reserve discipline.</b> Chapter 1 named the disease (custody spent); here is the structural cure: the tax custodies SEGREGATED in cash, not just in accounts — the tax-reserve bank account (module 3's purpose-per-account control, now with its most important tenant) receiving, on a rhythm, the cash matching the custody balances: the month's VAT position swept as it accrues or at close, the WHT deductions moved when withheld, the payroll custodies parked at payroll run. The mechanics are one contra entry per sweep (module 3's machinery); the discipline is the RULE that the reserve is not working capital — it is the state's money sitting in a room with the state's name on it — and the payoff is the module's promised boredom: remittance day is a transfer from a funded account, executed by the calendar, with no scramble, no bridging loan, and no conversation about which supplier waits so the state can be paid. The sizing honesty: firms under genuine cash strain will resist the sweep (<i>we need that float</i>) — and the consultant's answer is the framing that has closed this argument at every client who heard it: the custody money was never the firm's float; spending it is borrowing from the state at penalty-plus-interest rates without asking — the most expensive credit line in Nigeria, taken by default. A firm that cannot fund its reserve has a working-capital problem to solve honestly (the receivables discipline, the stock release ladder — tools three tracks supplied), not a tax float to burn.</p><p><b>The cash-flow read.</b> The calendar's obligations, projected: the rolling view of what falls due in the next 60-90 days against the reserve's funding — one line on the treasury read (module 3's daily position glance, extended), and the early-warning that converts the rare genuine squeeze from a deadline-day crisis into a planned conversation (the authority approached BEFORE the due date about instalments is a different meeting than the one after — the accountant leads it; the system's contribution is that the numbers were known in time to have it).</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Audits & the evidence posture", "13", "<p>Sooner or later the authority asks questions — the desk query, the reconciliation request, the field audit. The chair's preparation is not lawyering; it is a POSTURE, built monthly, and this chapter is its anatomy.</p><p><b>The posture in one sentence.</b> <b>Registers tied to accounts tied to documents, filed on time, evidenced in folders</b> — when that chain exists, every authority question is an extraction exercise; when it doesn't, every question is a reconstruction project, and reconstruction under deadline is where firms concede assessments they never owed. The posture is Chapter 6's evidence file compounding: twelve monthly folders per tax per year, each self-contained, and the audit support task collapsing to <i>which months, which taxes — here.</i></p><p><b>The encounter types, at working altitude.</b> The <b>desk review</b> — the authority's routine reconciliation: their records of your filings against their expectations (increasingly automated as the e-invoicing rails mature — Chapter 2's point that they now hold half the reconciliation; the firm whose register fed its returns matches by construction, and the mismatch letters go to the firms that filed parallel computations). The <b>query letter</b> — specific questions on specific periods: answered ON-RECORD (module 4's dispute hygiene, authority edition — the reply in writing, the evidence attached, the file noting what was sent when), within the stated window, factually and without volunteering adjacent essays (answer what was asked, completely; the accountant reviews before it goes). The <b>field audit</b> — the authority in the building: the preparation is the standing posture plus logistics (a room, a liaison — ONE person through whom requests flow, so the audit gets consistent, logged responses instead of hallway answers), and the consultant's role defined precisely: <b>produce evidence, not arguments</b> — the system's records extracted cleanly, the drill-downs demonstrated where they help, the POSITIONS argued by the accountant and the tax adviser, never by the consultant (Chapter 2's boundary, at its highest-stakes appearance).</p><p><b>The common findings, pre-empted.</b> What trading-firm audits actually find, each one a chapter of this academy running in reverse: output VAT on invoices absent from returns (the register-return divergence — the bridge skipped); input VAT claimed on defective invoices (the evidence discipline — Procurement 7's invoice-quality money lesson); WHT deducted but unremitted (the custody spent — the finding with interest attached); WHT remitted but unscheduled (the detail register absent — remittances the authority cannot attribute); PAYE understated against the payroll reality (the spreadsheet-beside-the-system disease); and related-party and cash transactions undocumented (the evidence posture's edges). The consultant reading this list correctly sees the module's rituals as the pre-emption of every line — which is the honest sales pitch for the whole module: the audit that ends early is not lucky; it is the monthly folders, met.</p><p><b>The posture's tone.</b> Last, the cultural note consultants transmit to clients: the relationship with the authority is long, and the firm's reputation with it is an asset — filings on time, replies on record, positions argued professionally through the right professionals, and the tax clearance certificate renewed as the routine it becomes. Firms that treat every interaction as combat buy attention; firms that treat compliance as the subscription it is buy quiet — and quiet, for a trading firm with margins to make, is worth more than any argument ever won.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Case study: HealthTrade's compliance year", "16", "<p>The silent partner, professionally held. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>The consolidation (Ch. 1, 3).</b> The chair's first month assembles what three tracks built: the four custody accounts confirmed and typed, the item masters' tax categories audited (the pharmacy's exempt basic-medicine lines vs standard-rated cosmetics — 14 items re-categorised, the mixed-supply registers now able to tell their streams apart, the apportionment question handed to the accountant WITH clean data), and the first full monthly VAT ritual run end to end: registers closed, both bridges tied to the kobo, the return prepared from the system, filed, remitted from the new reserve, <b>VAT Payable verified at zero</b>. The accountant's note in the file: <i>first month the return and the ledger were the same document.</i></p><p><b>The certificate chase (Ch. 4).</b> The WHT Receivable archaeology: the sufferer-side register reconstructed from allocation history finds <b>₦1.34m of deductions suffered over two years — certificates held for only ₦610k</b>. The chase instituted: outstanding certificates listed per customer, aged, worked like receivables (the corporate customers' finance teams contacted with the professional ask; the government customer escalated through its due process) — eight months later, certificates for a further <b>₦590k recovered</b> and handed to the accountant for the CIT credit: the firm's own tax, un-paid-twice, at the cost of a standing worklist. The remaining ₦140k — deductions by two defunct customers, certificates unobtainable — is written to the accountant's judgment with its file: even the loss is now a documented decision instead of a quiet leak.</p><p><b>The reserve's first test (Ch. 7).</b> Month four: a heavy import quarter squeezes cash, and the old instinct surfaces in a management meeting — <i>the VAT account has ₦2.1m sitting idle; borrow it for the Emzor payment, replace it by the 21st.</i> The framing does its work: the custody argument restated (the most expensive credit line in Nigeria, taken by default), the ALTERNATIVE worked instead — module 4's receivables push accelerates two collections; the stock ladder releases a slow line — and the reserve stays whole. Remittance day passes as a transfer, as designed; and the MD's conversion is complete by Q3, when the reserve sweep becomes the month's first standing transfer rather than its last argument.</p><p><b>The desk query (Ch. 8).</b> Month nine: a query letter — two months' VAT, the authority's e-invoice-fed expectation against the filed returns, one difference flagged. The evidence posture answers it in a day: the difference traced in the file to a credit note issued in month six (reducing output legitimately — the document, the register line, the return's treatment all agreeing), the reply drafted on-record with the evidence attached, the accountant's review, sent within the window. The authority's closure letter arrives in three weeks; the file gains its first external validation; and the office learns the lesson at the right price: <i>the folder answered before we finished worrying.</i></p><p><b>The transition navigated (Ch. 2, 5).</b> Across the year, the reform's working edges land as calendar items, not crises: the payroll setup re-based when the accountant confirms the new PAYE structure (the system applying the current bands the day they take effect; the multi-state split — Lagos and Ogun — running from the register's state column), the small-company eligibility question answered by the accountant with the system's turnover read as evidence, and the e-invoicing rail's expansion absorbed by a firm whose invoices already fed its returns. The year closes with the compliance calendar's every line green, the twelve monthly folders complete, the clearance certificate renewed routinely — and the module's cultural argument won in the accountant's year-end words: <i>tax used to be the thing we feared quarterly; now it's the thing we do monthly.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 10 — Common mistakes & the fifth law", "13", "<p>The tax layer's scar tissue — each pattern a firm somewhere is living right now. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — custody spent.</b> Symptom: remittance day as recurring crisis; the VAT account funded by scramble; interest and penalties as line items. Disease: the state's money used as float (Ch. 1, 7) — the most expensive credit line in Nigeria, taken by default. Fix: the reserve account with its sweep rhythm, the working-capital problem solved honestly with the academy's real tools, and the custody framing taught until it holds.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the parallel computation.</b> Symptom: returns prepared in spreadsheets beside the system; the filed numbers and the booked numbers diverging; nobody able to say which is true. Disease: two sources (Ch. 6) — the return no longer a restatement of the ledger. Fix: the return FROM the system, the registers' bridges as the pre-filing gate, and the spreadsheet retired to what it should be — a formatting tool, never a source.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the unreconciled registers.</b> Symptom: register totals and account movements that drift apart unexamined; the desk review's mismatch letter arriving as a surprise. Disease: the bridges skipped (Ch. 3, 6) — while the authority's e-invoice rails now run their half automatically. Fix: the register-to-account line in every monthly ritual, divergences worked before filing, and the zeroing invariant verified as each cycle's close.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — certificates uncollected.</b> Symptom: WHT suffered for years with a certificate file that fits in an envelope; the CIT bill paid gross. Disease: the sufferer side run on resignation (Ch. 4) — the firm's own tax paid twice, quietly. Fix: the deduction captured at allocation, the chase as a standing aged worklist, and the recovered credits handed to the accountant with their evidence — the module's most reliably fundable ritual.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — payroll beside the system.</b> Symptom: salaries in a spreadsheet, a monthly lump journal, PAYE computed elsewhere, state splits improvised, the annual return an archaeology project. Disease: the register that can produce any slice replaced by one that produces none (Ch. 5). Fix: payroll into the system (or rigorously bridged), the custody chain checked monthly, payslips as the standing employee certificate, and the annual return reduced to an extract.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — penalties as a cost of doing business.</b> Symptom: chronic lateness normalised; the clearance certificate perpetually delayed; the authority's attention earned and compounding. Disease: the calendar unowned (Ch. 6-7) — compliance experienced as weather instead of run as a rhythm. Fix: the consolidated calendar with names, the reserve making funding a non-event, and the cultural reset the case study modelled — from feared quarterly to done monthly.</p><p><b>The fifth law.</b> The chart is the language; the journal is the exception; the bank is the outside witness; the controls tie or nothing is trusted. The fifth names the silent partner's terms: <b>tax accounts are custody, not cost — the state's money is held in its own accounts, reconciled to its registers, remitted on the calendar, and evidenced forever.</b> Both VAT sides joined monthly, both WHT directions worked, the payroll chain tied, the six-step ritual zeroing every custody, the reserve keeping the money where the name is, and the evidence file answering every question in a day. Module 6 turns from what the firm owes to where the firm earns: cost centers and dimensions — the machinery that gives profit an address.</p>"),
]

